## 4. Control Plane High Availability

A highly available Kubernetes control plane uses multiple control-plane instances so that the failure of one instance does not necessarily stop cluster management.

Conceptual architecture:

```text
              kubectl
                 |
          Load Balancer
                 |
        +--------+--------+
        |        |        |
       CP1      CP2      CP3
        |        |        |
        +--------+--------+
                 |
              Workers
                 |
           Application Pods
```

A production-ready highly available control plane also requires appropriate API server endpoints, etcd availability, and failure-domain planning.

### KOPS control-plane scaling

Inspect the instance groups first:

```bash
kops get ig --name reyaz.k8s.local
```

Edit the relevant control-plane instance group only after confirming that your KOPS version and cluster configuration support the intended topology.

Apply and validate changes:

```bash
kops update cluster --name reyaz.k8s.local
kubectl get nodes -o wide
```

**Important:** Do not assume that changing `minSize` and `maxSize` alone creates a complete highly available control plane. A production topology must account for availability zones, load balancing, and etcd quorum. For three etcd voting members, a majority of two must remain available.

### Node failure and recovery

If a machine is managed by an appropriate Auto Scaling Group, AWS may replace a terminated instance. However, Kubernetes must still recognize the replacement, and Pods must be rescheduled successfully.

Deleting a node is not the same as safely draining it. For planned maintenance, use:

```bash
kubectl drain NODE_NAME --ignore-daemonsets
kubectl uncordon NODE_NAME
```

Use these commands carefully and check Pod disruption budgets and storage constraints before draining a production node.

