## 5. Kubernetes Deployments

A Deployment manages replicated application Pods through ReplicaSets.

Example: `deployment.yml`

```yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: ib-deployment
  labels:
    app: bank
spec:
  replicas: 4
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: bank
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: bank
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: cont1
          image: trainerreyaz/ib-image:latest
```

Create or update the Deployment:

```bash
kubectl apply -f deployment.yml
kubectl get deployments
kubectl get pods -o wide
kubectl get nodes -o wide
```

The `-o wide` option displays additional information, including the node hosting each Pod.

### Scale out Pods

```bash
kubectl scale deployment/ib-deployment --replicas=10
kubectl get pods -o wide
```

### Scale in Pods

```bash
kubectl scale deployment/ib-deployment --replicas=4
kubectl get pods -o wide
```

### Important scheduling concept

Kubernetes schedules ordinary application Pods onto eligible nodes according to resource availability, scheduling constraints, and policies. Control-plane nodes are often tainted to prevent ordinary workloads from running on them, but this is configurable.

Kubernetes does not guarantee that Pods will be distributed equally across workers. Use topology spread constraints or pod anti-affinity when placement across nodes or zones is important.

### Example: Mobile banking Deployment

Save this as `deploynew.yml`:

```yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: mb-deployment
  labels:
    app: bank
spec:
  replicas: 4
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: bank
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: bank
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: cont1
          image: trainerreyaz/mb-image:latest
```

Apply and verify:

```bash
kubectl apply -f deploynew.yml
kubectl get pods -o wide
kubectl scale deployment/mb-deployment --replicas=10
```

