## 10. Immutable Infrastructure

Immutable infrastructure means replacing infrastructure components with newly built versions instead of manually modifying existing components in place.

### Traditional versus immutable

| Traditional approach | Immutable approach |
|---|---|
| Modify an existing server | Build and replace with a new server |
| Install changes manually | Automate configuration and deployment |
| Risk of configuration drift | More repeatable environments |
| Rollback can be difficult | Rollback can use a previous known-good version |

### Examples

- Docker: Build and deploy a new image.
- Kubernetes: Roll out a new Pod template and replace Pods.
- AWS: Build a new AMI and launch replacement instances.
- Terraform: Update infrastructure declaratively, replacing resources when required.

Kubernetes Deployments support controlled application rollouts. They do not make every Kubernetes resource or every infrastructure component inherently immutable.

### Benefits

- Consistent deployments.
- Easier rollback.
- Less configuration drift.
- Better automation.
- More repeatable environments.

