## 11. Idempotency

An operation is idempotent when repeating it produces the same intended result as performing it once.

Example:

```bash
kubectl apply -f deployment.yml
```

Running this repeatedly converges the managed resource toward the configuration declared in the manifest, assuming the configuration and external conditions remain unchanged.

Another example is a configuration-management task that ensures a user exists rather than blindly attempting to create a new user every time.

### Idempotent versus non-idempotent

| Idempotent operation | Non-idempotent example |
|---|---|
| Ensure a directory exists | Append the same line every run without checking |
| Ensure a service is enabled | Create a new resource every run without checking |
| Apply a Kubernetes manifest | Increment a counter each time |

Idempotency is especially useful in automation, configuration management, and CI/CD pipelines because repeated runs become more predictable.

